<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bishapur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bishapur was a city in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> founded by the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> in the third century CE. Its construction drew on the labour and engineering skills of Roman prisoners captured in Shapur's campaigns. The city shows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>'s deliberate use of captured expertise in building and infrastructure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bishapur.jpg" alt="Bishapur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Behnam9395 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anahita_Temple_of_Bishapur_(Kazerun)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bishapur lies beside the Chowgan Valley and the Shapur River, 15 kilometres west of Kazerun in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, near a river crossing. Its name combines a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> word meaning great or lord with the name of its founder. The city was laid out on a rectangular plan about 2 kilometres long and 1 kilometre wide, with two main streets, one running north-south and one east-west, meeting at the centre and ending at four gates. Its plan follows the Greek system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodamus-of-miletus">Hippodamus</a>, which makes it unusual among the ancient cities of Iran. It had a royal citadel and a common citadel, the latter with houses, a bathhouse, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> and a market.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to an inscription, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> founded the city in 266 CE. He had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> three times, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii">Gordian III</a>, capturing the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> and forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-arab">Philip the Arab</a> to surrender. Archaeologists have found Parthian and Elamite remains on the site. Roman prisoners are said to have built the city, and its architect, according to the inscription on one of two commemorative columns, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apasa">Apasa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>. Valerian lived in a palace built for him there. Two Sasanian kings, Hormizd I and Bahram I, were born in Bishapur, and before the Arab conquest it had between 50,000 and 80,000 inhabitants.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took it in 638 CE, and the people of the province rose against them several times, in 643, 646 and 687. In the 8th and 9th centuries its prosperity passed to Kazerun as residents moved there. The Buyid rulers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, revived it for a time. In 495 AH Abu Sa'id Shabankara set fire to the city and razed it, and an earthquake then completed its ruin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bishapur was entered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s national heritage list in 1931 and on the World Heritage List in 2018. Its monuments include the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>, a cube about 14 metres on a side built so that water from the Shapur River ran through it; the Ceremonial Hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, a cross-shaped hall of 781 square metres under a dome; the octagonal Valerian Palace; and the mosaic iwan. Georges Salles and Roman Ghirshman excavated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> between 1935 and 1940, and an Iranian team led by Ali Akbar Sarfaraz began work 28 years later. Only about three percent of the city has been uncovered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bishapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shapur's foundation of <b>Bishapur</b> and the Band-e Kaisar dam across the Karun River at Shushtar are both associated with Roman captive labour and expertise, and show how captured knowledge was deliberately put to use.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
